{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788201769,"nonce":"x7qWt2eXZVNd6ssUBLrlx5qJj48=","message":"VERIFICATION (backlog verification pass, git-bug 2f2796e, run fbb2c59)\n\nCONFIRMED — both amendments hold, including the drop-pod detail. Two additions,\none of which settles the open question the issue says must not be left open.\n\nWHAT I CONFIRMED BY READING\n\n1. `ScenPart_PlayerPawnsArriveMethod.DoDropPods` really does pass `forbid: true`.\n   Verbatim, from the last line of that member:\n\n       DropPodUtility.DropThingGroupsNear(MapGenerator.PlayerStartSpot, map, list, 110,\n           Find.GameInitData.QuickStarted || method != PlayerPawnsArriveMethod.DropPods,\n           leaveSlag: true, canRoofPunch: true, forbid: true, allowFogged: false);\n\n   CONFIRMED. And the tutorial agrees: `Data/Core/Defs/Tutor/Instructions.xml`\n   lists `UnforbidStartingResources` third, immediately after `MakeStockpile` /\n   `EndStockpileDesignating`. Both halves of that amendment paragraph are right.\n\n2. \"~54 vanilla windows force-pause\". CONFIRMED to the nearest honest number:\n   `grep -rl \"forcePause = true\"` over the decompiled 1.6 tree returns\n   **54 files**. That is a file count, not a class count — a file may hold more\n   than one type and some set it conditionally — so \"~54\" is the right way to\n   state it and it should keep the tilde.\n\n3. 1.7 shipped and behaves as the session-5 amendment says: `TimeDriver` halts\n   with `Halt(\"dialog\", ForcePausePayload(stack), ...)` and publishes\n   `data[\"halted_on\"]`. CONFIRMED.\n\n4. \"Cook is unreachable without production bills.\" CONFIRMED by enumeration\n   rather than by inference. The shipped verb set contains `bills` (a READ —\n   `ColonyVerbs.Bills`) and `surgery-add` / `surgery-remove` (MEDICAL bills only,\n   `MedicalBillVerbs`). There is no verb that adds a production bill to a bench.\n   So the amendment's \"either take the dep, or state plainly that M1 runs on\n   stocked and grown food\" is a real fork, not a hedge.\n\nADDITION 1 — THE OPEN QUESTION ABOUT `dev:starter-kit` AND FORBIDDING IS ALREADY\nANSWERED BY THE CODE, AND THE ANSWER IS \"NO, IT DOES NOT MIMIC A LANDING.\"\nThe amendment says \"Either answer is fine; leaving it undecided is not\", so here\nis the fact it needs: `Source/AutoRimmer/StarterKit.cs` contains **no reference\nto forbidding at all** — a grep for `forbid`/`Forbid` over that file returns\nnothing. Everything `dev:starter-kit` conjures arrives unforbidden. So today M1\nprovably does not rehearse the drop-pod obstacle, and it is not a matter of\nopinion.\n\nMy recommendation, and it is a recommendation not a finding: **make it mimic.**\nThe argument that decides it is not \"realism\" but the one DESIGN already makes\nabout fog — an agent handed information or affordances no player has weakens the\ncolony as a test substrate. A forbidden starting kit is the ONLY thing on the M1\npath that exercises `unforbid` against a real obstacle, `unforbid` is already\nshipped, and comment #5 on 4.1 (96d9315) records Evan catching the same class of\nfailure live: a forbidden rifle, revolver, knife and flak set sitting unused\nwhile FSWA silently skipped them (`FSWA_MapComponent` checks\n`!thing.IsForbidden(Faction.OfPlayer)` in three separate places). If M1 never\nstarts forbidden, that failure mode cannot appear in a ten-day run, and it is\ndemonstrably one that occurs. Cost: one flag on the kit and one checklist line.\n\nADDITION 2 — THE DEPENDENCY LINE IS STILL WRONG IN THE BODY, TWO AMENDMENTS LATER.\nBoth amendments say so and neither edits it; comment #0 still reads\n`Depends: 2.1, 2.2, 2.3, 3.1, 3.2, 3.4, 4.1, 4.2 (3.3 optional)`. A worker\nreading top-down gets the stale list and has to reach comment #2 to learn that\n3.5 is a hard dependency. That is the same shape as 3.5's own stale acceptance\nsection (flagged there) and it is worth fixing in the body rather than only in\ncomments, because the dependency line is what the muster reads.\n\nThe verified list, for whoever edits it: add **1.7 (done)** and **3.5 (open,\n`20e5cda`)**; decide 3.6 (`48f666c`) explicitly one way or the other; 3.3\n(`1adc737`) stays optional but note that 3.3 is itself blocked on 1.9\n(`acee526`), so \"optional\" is doing real work here.\n\nACCEPTANCE — TESTABLE, AND THE ONE HARD GATE IS CORRECTLY MARKED\n\n- \"≥2 of 3 colonists alive at day 10 without post-staging dev intervention\" is\n  checkable from the journal, which records dev-verb provenance — that is the\n  right proof and the invariant already names it.\n- \"Dorian signs off ... (hard gate — orchestrator stops here)\" is correctly\n  labelled as a human gate. Good; 4.2's \"reviewed against the skill text for\n  compliance\" should be labelled the same way and is not.\n- One clause that is not yet checkable: \"the clothes check firing at least once\n  (verify via transcript)\". Whether it fires depends on biome and season, and the\n  fixture is only specified as \"temperate\". Worth pinning the season or the\n  landing tile so the run is not a coin flip on one of its named criteria. NOTE\n  from 4.1's verification that bears directly on this: `Alert_NeedWarmClothes`\n  counts only apparel for which `IsInAnyStorage()` holds, so on a start with gear\n  on the ground the alert can fire with warm clothes twenty cells away — which,\n  if Addition 1 is taken, is a feature: the clothes check and the unforbid\n  obstacle become the same rehearsal.\n- \"zero unexplained red errors (each red error ... triaged: ours vs\n  mod-under-test vs vanilla)\" is testable and is the right formulation — it\n  budgets for reality without weakening the invariant.\n\nWHAT I AM INFERRING, NOT CONFIRMING\n\n- That a ten-day run WILL hit a timed letter. That is a probability claim about\n  the storyteller, not something I read. It is very likely and I did not verify\n  it; nothing in the amendments' reasoning depends on the exact rate.\n- The recommendation in Addition 1. The FACT (starter-kit does not forbid) is\n  read from the file; the \"make it mimic\" call is mine.","files":null}]}